At age 14, I read this book with an attitude of "I'll just read it for fun." I thought that such books wouldn't be able to get me addicted because I've never been attracted to web novels from Startling Stories before.
And then ... it fell down in the hole.
According to this story, most of the book is quite light-hearted and lighthearted. In the earlier part where Ye Xiaoxiao was still gaming in the group, even if there were factional intrigues between groups, because they often got bloodied at Ye Sheng’s hands, it made them not very heavy.
And in the part about the competition, I wrote down some feelings of nostalgia for the passage of time and the old-new transition (perhaps because I am melancholic).
Reading the whole book took me much of my senior high school study time but I couldn't stop reading it.
Full-time dedication to character and emotional arcs captivates readers deeply, with characters who barely appear having significant impressions; notable roles such as Xie Ye, Huang Shoutian, and Su Mo’er have numerous fans. Characters that don’t make the writing center are equally popular, such as Jiang Bopao and Chu Yunxiu, who are very easy to like in an e-sports novel where there is no extreme dislikeable person. This fully demonstrates the success of full-time in character depiction.
And on the emotional front, full-time has no of course problems with the afterglow girls in general web novels that have been criticized for being either beautiful or perverted; all the characters’ emotions share one common feature – they like Glory and most want to win honorably.
Small measures have no great conspiracy; everyone looks so simple and adorable.
This may be why there are no unpleasant roles for full-time work.
Full-time IP is huge, and this is also to thank for it.
Forget about web fiction as a cultural phenomenon and Full Time is still an excellent read.
How much of him really gives some blood and a little bit of emotion to people?
This year in April, full-time anime will be made.
