A while ago I read and just happened to see something scribbled from memory about some reflections on my reading.
The book is more of a collection of fragments from life regarding love than a short story collection.
The tone of this book is extremely mild and mundane – like drinking plain water – to an extreme level of brevity and economy in choosing words and sentences, as well as in constructing the plot, which resembles a quiet street on a summer afternoon.
Every short story is a small snapshot of time from ordinary life, with no beginning and no end.
Finally gathered together, entitled "When We Talk About Love, What Are We Talking About?"
I think this novel collection, and its title, is akin to some kind of performance art; the author wants to express that love is not limited to the ups-and-downs of people on stage, or to the immovable rocks in the sea. Love does not have to be about those who are so attached to each other like glue; love also can’t just be limited to above; love should also represent the simplicity of everyday life, the tediousness of day-to-day routines without any purpose, the helplessness towards life itself, the avoidance from reality, and the uncertainty over the future.
As you read this book, memories of arguing couples beside you, uninteresting marriages with acquaintances, and unsatisfactory relationships may come to mind.
This is precisely why I do not recommend this book: from an aesthetic standpoint, the style and plot of the novel are unremarkable; unless your life is like raging waters or as sweet as living in a candy castle, you will find yourself feeling bored when you read it.
And in terms of depth of thought, everyday situations do not surprise or expand one’s mind and perspective (this is unlike in Gabriel Garcia Marquez’s Love in the Time of Cholera), while brief episodes fail to address any issues, such as what kind of mistake led to the failure of love?
What causes the breakdown of the protagonist's marriage?
So I am not going to recommend reading this book; whether or not you read it doesn’t make much difference.
Author's ideas are expressed more through skills and form than plot, which is also the reason for a poorer reading experience with this book.
